How the Government Could Use FFP to Spur True Agentic AI Innovation

Odd right?  A firm fixed price contract could be the catalyst to spur agentic AI growth.  And the U.S. government is in a prime position to push it to the next level - not private Industry! It could potentially lead to a switch from the request for proposal model (tell us what you can do to solve a problem at your price) to firm fixed price model(here is what we want, exactly, specifically at this set price).  HHere is how.  

The government establish a pilot project.  The project contains a set of open source, unclassified FFP software needs.  The needs are defined by government analysts and software engineers.  They minimally contain the following:

- An open source data set of interest (at least initially),
- Aa set of use cases and requirements for software that uses the data,
- A cloud environment developers may work in,
- Alimited budget via OpenRouter for any model developers wish to use,
- An API and interfaces for the developed software,
- A set of unit and functional tests,
- A target deployment environment (the hardware, processor, etc.) where the finished product will live,
- Target performance metrics,
- Security tests and harnesses, and 
- A hidden evaluation set to test against.

The here is thelast piece of the puzzle, the firm fixed price.  But the government makes the price very budget conscious.  Ridiculously budget conscious.  If an engineering team that assembled the above specification were to look at the requirements and say "We could not possibly do this for less than $250K even with agentic AI." - then set the price at $150Kk.  Set it 40% lower.

Why?  The agentic AI tools exist to streamline software development.  The results are there.  What isn't there is the motivation to really streamline them.  The FFP is the motivation to truly innovate their use. And let's face it, a majority of engineering is simply motivating the best engineers to be creative!  To do more, to do better, with less in a constrained environment.

The FFP approach poses this question to enigneers - 'Are you really that good?'  Consider a project that has a $250K estimate.  Could a team of three engineers take agentic AI, create skills, apply them successfully, innovate the application of AI to get a dependable and reliable solution up and running in five weeks where they each get $25K?  Consider, if these same three engineers perform at peak efficiency for just 20 weeks of the year - they would net $200K. 

Tell me this would not stimulate innovation of how to use and apply agentic AI tools.  The potential is there, but the model to incentivize what could be done really does not exist yet. 

Genesis of Idea
This idea was partially motivated by my recent reading of the book Crisis in Bethlehem.  It doucments the decline of Bethlem Steel from teh worlds #2 steel provider in 1948 to barely clining to life by 1986.  Many factors contributed to this, one was having a beheamoth organization when technologies and trends naturally sought out more nimble methods.

Bethlehem's Fabricated Steel Construction is an example.  This was Bethlehems flagship division, "builders of such national landmarks as the George Washington and Golden Gate Brides, and of such famed office buildings as Chicago's Merchandise Mart and New York's Waldorf-Astoria." It was a massive and impressive operation, composed of engineers who created the designs and fabrictors who buld them.  A soup-to-nuts shop who could serve as an erector that was dependable and prodcuedd on schedule.  But, simply became too expensive.

Advances in design techniques, proliferation of design knowledge and expertise, and runaway construction costs led to wiser purchasers.  They began to demand FFP contracts to reduce costs.  

The World Trade Center, oddly, was the last straw for Bethlehem's Fabricated Steel Construction.  Bethlehme offered a bid of $117M for procurement and fabrication of the structural steel for the towers.  They beat out U.S. Steels bid of $122M, incidentally.  The NY Port Authority looked at the estimate and believed it was too high.  Way too high.  They had an estimate of $80M.  Though technially the lowest bidder, the Port Authority rejected the bid.  What did they do instead?  They changed the acauisiton structure.  

Malcom Levy, the Port Authority's chief of planning and constuction broke the fabricating work into smaller segments.  These were very specfic contracts with a limited scope, similar to an FFP [cite this:  https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-C13-a05e00c5b8c7e83d409fe3a5ea5a3368/pdf/GOVPUB-C13-a05e00c5b8c7e83d409fe3a5ea5a3368.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com] The contract read very specfic on what is wanted and needed. 

The end result?  Bids came in from smaller and ephemral teams across six different cotnracts.  They totalled $80M.  Actualy costs for the constuction and fabrication ended up at $83M.  That's around 37% less than the Bethlemhem bid. The U.S. government now has an opportunity to see these same cost gains with software development.  



Why buy the industry's traditional packaged solution if we can decompose the requirement and create a different competitive market for the underlying capability?




